tierB = {name:"Pro", attributes:["$29 / seat / mo","Unlimited projects","100 GB storage","Priority support + SSO"]}
tierC = {name:"Team", attributes:["$59 / seat / mo","Unlimited + audit log","1 TB storage","Dedicated CSM"]}
tbl1 = Table("Cost at 10 seats", ["Tier","Monthly","Yearly (2 months free)"], [["Starter","$90","$900"],["Pro","$290","$2,900"],["Team","$590","$5,900"]])
m1 = Metric("Best value", "Pro", "Unlimited projects, SSO and 100 GB storage at 3x Starter's price", "positive")
:::

**Pro wins on value** — it's the only tier where the price jump buys a capability change rather than more of the same. Starter's hard project limit and lack of SSO are the two things teams typically outgrow first, so the $20/seat step up usually pays for itself in avoided migration work.

**Step up to Team only if** you need audit logs or a compliance story — the extra $30/seat buys governance, not productivity. If you're a small team under 5 people and don't need SSO, Starter is defensible; anything larger, jump straight to Pro.

