cmp1 = Comparison("AWS vs Azure vs Google Cloud", [a1, a2, a3])
a1 = {name:"AWS", attributes:["Broadest catalog (~200+ services)","Biggest ecosystem, docs & talent pool","Most regions and edge locations","Pricing flexible but complex"]}
a2 = {name:"Azure", attributes:["Best fit for Microsoft / .NET / Entra ID","Strongest hybrid story (Arc, Stack)","Powerful enterprise agreements","Portal & naming can feel inconsistent"]}
a3 = {name:"Google Cloud", attributes:["Leads on data & AI (BigQuery, Vertex)","Kubernetes birthplace (GKE)","Simplest, most predictable pricing","Smallest ecosystem and services breadth"]}
ch1 = Chart("bar", "Rough share of cloud infrastructure spend", ["AWS", "Azure", "GCP"], [{name:"Share (%)", values:[30, 25, 12]}])
:::

**How to choose in practice:** default to **AWS** if you need the widest service selection, the deepest hiring pool, or the most mature third-party integrations. Choose **Azure** if you're already invested in Microsoft — Active Directory/Entra ID, Windows Server, Office 365 licensing and enterprise agreements make it the cheapest path to production. Choose **Google Cloud** if your workload is data- or AI-centric (BigQuery, Vertex AI) or heavily Kubernetes-based, and you value a simpler bill over the broadest catalog.

In practice most organisations end up multi-cloud: one primary provider plus a specialty workload elsewhere — and the egress fees between them are the hidden cost worth modelling before you commit.
